  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 PM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nA pleasant fall afternoon once again, through a touch breezy with \nsouthwesterly wind gusts of 20-25 mph. The pressure gradient has \nincreased in response to low pressure over the Hudson Bay and \npersistent high pressure over the Carolinas. This low has a trailing \ncold front nosing into northern Illinois this afternoon. Abundant \ndry air precludes any rain chances with this front. Cooler tomorrow, \nespecially downwind of Lake Michigan, yet southern areas will remain \npleasant in the mid-70s. \n\nTurning to Friday and the weekend, increasing opportunities for rain \nas the jet stream buckles in response to a Pacific Northwest trough. \nThe downstream ridge amplifies over the eastern seaboard sending a \nsurge of warmth poleward for the weekend. Amid this pattern change, \na modest warm front may serve as the focus for isolated showers over \nIllinois as early as Friday. We retain a dry forecast at this time \nwith the best focus for lift a moisture colocated to our west, yet \nthis will be a modest feature to keep an eye on.\n\nOf greater interest is Tropical Storm Isaias, which is forecast to be \nswept northward in the coming days. This weekend, the primary \nsteering mechanism for this system will be the incoming Pacific \nNorthwest trough and a subtropical ridge over the mid-Atlantic \ncoast. There continues to be some disagreement in the east-west \nnature of the track of Isaias, and so our forecast continues to \ncarry a 40-70% chance of rain as early as Saturday night \nlingering into Sunday, with the greatest rain chances well away \nfrom Lake Michigan. Significant impacts are not expected. \n\nAnother area of low pressure tracks through the Midwest later Monday \nand Tuesday, representing our next chance for rain, with continued \nfluxuations in daily high temperatures.",
